# Server — the HTTP surface the visualizer and hosted deployments talk to.

import "code_graph.elv"

c server {
    d: "Axum HTTP surface over an analyzed graph, in two deployment shapes that share the handlers: one local root served from disk, and a hosted multi-repo instance that clones on demand."
    cr: "src/server/"
    f local_serve
    f hosted_repos
    f graph_api
    f scope_api
    f access_control
    f activity_stream
    f spec_authoring
}

f spec_authoring {
    d: "The one route that writes the repo's own prose, and the ceiling that makes it safe to put behind a button: POST /api/spec/entity CREATES one Elevator entity and can do nothing else — never a rewrite, never a delete. A `.elv` is prose somebody wrote, and an endpoint that can only add a definition is one a click can be wired to. Two edits land in one save: the definition appended at the end of the file, and a child reference inserted into the parent's body so the entity is filed rather than orphaned. Written into the PARENT's own file, which is what keeps `import` bookkeeping out of it — a child reference across files needs one, and adding imports on the author's behalf would make every click a two-file edit. The parent arrives as an ID and never as a path, because the two spellings of a path here disagree: the in-memory graph holds the analyzer's absolute paths while /api/graph has been relativized by the renderer, and the first end-to-end run 400'd on every parented write until parent_file derived both. The anchor for the child insertion is the parent's SPAN, which is a claim about the text as the analyzer last read it, so insert_child_ref verifies the block still opens with the keyword and name the graph says before touching it; a file edited since is written to unparented with a note, because an orphan is a --check hint while a child line in the wrong Category is a wrong map. Nothing is invented for an empty field — a body-less `f name` is a complete definition and the guide's recommended first move, and the alternative is a plausible sentence nobody wrote in a file whose whole worth is that every sentence was. spec_write.rs is pure text and holds every decision worth arguing about; the handler resolves the parent, checks the name is free, and does the reading and writing. Watch mode only: `mezz serve` analyzes a tree a stranger's URL named (ADR 0008) and must not reach a path that writes to it."
    cr: "src/server/spec_handler.rs", "src/server/spec_write.rs"
}

f activity_stream {
    d: "What the engine is doing, as something other than stderr. Until this the only channel out of a running pass was the terminal that launched `mezz watch`, and the SSE stream carried nothing but completion pings — so a diff that takes over a minute on a large repository left every browser watching it with no evidence anything was happening, and the parse warnings that explain which files are MISSING from the graph were readable only by whoever owned that terminal. activity::Sink holds a bounded ring of Notices, one Running slot and a broadcast; the free functions print exactly the string they record, so no command's terminal output changes and a process with no sink installed just prints as before. The sink is a process global as well as an AppState field, because the loudest messages come from inside Analyzer::parse_files — several frames below anything that has heard of a server, inside a rayon parallel iterator, under a Config that is Serialize and cannot carry a trait object. Run is an RAII guard rather than a begin/end pair: compute_diff_blocking returns early from a dozen places, and every one of them would otherwise leave a browser showing a diff that stopped as still running, which is worse than showing nothing. The running slot is depth-counted rather than phase-matched, since runs overlap — the watcher re-analyzes while a diff analyzes its two sides — so a Step updates whatever is open whichever phase it names, and only the last End closes it. Warnings are capped per run (WARNINGS_PER_RUN) before broadcast and summarised at the close: one diff of a large tree repeats the same parse failure once per worktree, and the terminal keeps all of them either way. sse_handler selects over both channels and sends the activity frame with its payload INLINE, unlike the three pings that tell a client to come and get it — a round trip per status line would be a request every few hundred milliseconds for the length of a pass. Each frame carries the Running slot it produced, so no client re-derives it. activity_handler answers GET /api/activity?since= from the ring, for the two moments the stream cannot cover: a page opened mid-run, and one reconnecting after a drop."
    cr: "src/activity.rs"
}

f local_serve {
    d: "`mezz serve` / `mezz watch`: analyze one root, hold it in AppState, serve the UI and stream changes over SSE. spawn_file_watcher re-analyzes on save, gated by is_source_extension and has_source_change so editor noise and non-source writes do not trigger a pass; it takes a second watched root when spec_dir sits outside the first, a spec being something people edit. handle_reanalysis publishes the watched root's config but reads the analysis scope back from the shared one, or the next keystroke would silently undo a narrowing the reader made from the browser. build_router wires the routes; write_json_with_cancel lets an in-flight response abandon work when a newer analysis supersedes it. resolve_startup checks the flags that can fail before any analysis begins, so a typo costs a second rather than a full parse."
    cr: "src/server/mod.rs", "src/server/state.rs"
}

f access_control {
    d: "Who may talk to the server, and where its UI comes from. Both servers bind loopback, which is no boundary against a browser on the same machine, so AccessPolicy replaces permissive CORS with an allowlist: same-origin and vscode-webview:// always, --allow-origin by name, nothing else. needs_token adds a per-process pairing token on top for origins that are neither loopback nor the webview, since allowlisting a host trusts everything served from it; require_token accepts it as a Bearer header or a query parameter, because EventSource cannot set headers. hello_route sits outside both layers and answers everyone, so a refused page can find out it was refused rather than reading a CORS block as a dead port. ui_dir::resolve finds the built UI by flag, env, executable sibling, then working directory, so an installed binary is not stranded without the repo. require_trusted_ui is the SECOND bar, for the routes that change the host rather than read it — spawning an agent and writing a `.elv` — and it exists because neither half of the policy above is right for a mutation: the loopback exemption was traded for reading a graph and would hand any page the user has open a write to their repo, and the token middleware waves through requests with no Origin at all, which is every non-browser client. So the bar is 'a page this engine served, or someone holding the token', and it is stated ONCE rather than per route: the first copy was written inline in the agent handler, and a second copy in the spec-writing one would be a second place for the two to drift apart."
    cr: "src/server/access.rs", "src/server/ui_dir.rs"
}

f hosted_repos {
    d: "The multi-tenant shape: a RepoRegistry of RepoSlot entries keyed by slug (is_valid_slug gates the path segment), each with a JobStatus and a StatusEvent stream. parse_github_url accepts the public forms, jobs.rs clones under JobConfig limits — a concurrency JobLimiter, wait_with_timeout, dir_size_bytes as a size ceiling, and sanitize_git_stderr so clone failures cannot echo a token back. Analyzed graphs persist as zstd snapshots (ZSTD_LEVEL) written through write_atomic and rehydrated on restart; unsafe_passes_allowed keeps opt-in passes like trace_exact off in the hosted path."
    cr: "src/server/serve.rs", "src/server/repo.rs", "src/server/jobs.rs"
}

f graph_api {
    d: "The endpoints the canvas reads: graph_handler for nodes and edges, index_handler and details_handler for lazy per-entity detail, commits_handler over git_commits, stashes_handler over git_stashes, and the diff endpoints (diff_handler, set_root_handler, base_details_handler) that drive comparison against a ref. /api/analysis/scope re-runs the analyzer under a new language set, docs switch or spec directory; build_config_with_scope validates before anything expensive, since the walker's fallback lands on a stderr the browser never shows. Its spec_dir is tri-state — absent leaves it, empty clears it, a path sets it — and may name a directory outside the root, which the settings file's key may not: this request came from a page the operator opened, not from a file that arrived with a clone. Educator hits the same router through position_handler, scan_handler and diagnostics_handler. parse_stash_lines is split out of git_stashes and splits each row from BOTH ends — a stash subject embeds a commit subject, which is free text and may hold the separator — and takes the first %P entry as the base, the second being the stashed index and the third the untracked files. It asks for no %gd: with --date=short set for %ad that placeholder renders as stash@{2026-08-12} instead of stash@{0}, so the selector is numbered by row position, which is all it ever was. No serve-mode twin of /api/stashes, unlike commits: serve mode exposes no POST /api/diff, so a stash listing there would be a list of comparisons nothing can run. Beside the analysis\'s reading of a change sit two endpoints that report the same change as GIT sees it, for f.diff_reading.files: changed_files_handler joins git diff --name-status -z with --numstat -z and adds untracked paths under a working head, and file_diff_handler hands back both sides of one file as text. Neither touches the analyzer, creates a worktree or reads the live graph, so both answer while a diff is still computing, and both take the same WORKING / STAGED sentinels diff_handler defined — head_side resolves them, and the index is read as the index (--cached, git show :path) rather than as the unreferenced commit staged_commit manufactures, which keeps the answer valid after that commit is collected. A comparison can also be called back once it has started: POST /api/diff/cancel sets diff_cancel, which diff::analyze_with_cancel threads into the per-file poll the analyzer already had, so a stop lands INSIDE the two analyses rather than at the end of them — the seams between are covered by stop_requested. It is a separate endpoint from DELETE /api/diff and means a different thing: that one leaves diff mode and drops the overlay, this one ends the computation and leaves whatever the reader was already looking at, which is the whole reason to stop rather than to clear. The flag is cleared as a run STARTS, under the diff_in_progress lock the cancel handler also takes, so a stop that lands in the moment one run is finishing cannot abort the next; a stop that arrives too late for any checkpoint is honoured instead by discarded, which keeps the finished answer off a canvas whose reader has withdrawn the question. A stopped run comes back through NoDiff::Declined — a stop is an answer, not a failure — and drop_worktrees removes the checkouts it made, since the alternative is a temp directory that grows by one worktree per impatient reader. The request is deliberately NOT aborted client-side: axum drops a handler whose connection closes, and this one clears diff_in_progress after its await, so an aborted fetch would leave the lock set with nobody to release it and every later comparison refused as already running."
    cr: "src/server/handlers.rs", "src/server/diff_handler.rs", "src/server/files_handler.rs", "src/server/analysis_handler.rs", "src/server/educator_handler.rs", "src/server/types.rs"
}

f scope_api {
    d: "Assembles a reading scope for an agent or the extension: collect_scope_manual takes an explicit entity set, collect_scope_directed expands from a seed under a traversal profile — refactor_rules follows callers and callees for change-blast questions, understand_rules follows definitions for reading. Returns ScopeEntity bodies with ScopeExports, and read_full_files decides where a whole file beats a stitched set of spans."
    cr: "src/server/scope_handler.rs"
}
